## Cross-cutting design

**State reset.** Every method keeps all state (parent/size arrays, visited/state arrays, heaps, adjacency)
in local variables created per request. There are no static fields, so one request cannot influence the next.
Checked by `python tools/run.py --check` ("state reset" case) and by `stateResetTest` in `AlgorithmsTests`.

**Deep DFS.** Recursion would overflow the JVM stack on a chain of 100 000 vertices. All DFS passes
(SCC pass 1 and 2, topo, DAG cycle check) use an explicit `int[] stack` plus a per-vertex edge pointer `pos[v]`,
which reproduces the "enter / next neighbour / finish" behaviour of recursive DFS. Each vertex is pushed at most
once, so the stack array of size `n` is enough. `find` in DSU is also iterative (two loops: find root, compress).
Checked by deep-chain tests (n = 100 000) for dsu, scc, topo and dag.

**Graph storage.** Compressed adjacency lists (`start[]`, `target[]`, `edgeId[]`) built with counting: O(n + m) time
and memory, no per-edge objects. `edgeId` keeps the *original* position in `edges`, which the output requires.

**Integers.** Totals and distances are `long` (up to 250 000 · 10⁹ ≈ 2.5·10¹⁴ > 2³¹). Weights are read as `int`.

## DSU (`dsu`)

*Steps.* parent[i]=i, size[i]=1; for each `[u,v]`: find both roots (path compression), if different attach the
smaller tree under the larger, update `components--` and `largest = max(largest, size[root])`; append `[components, largest]`
(also for self-unions and repeats, where nothing changes).
*Invariants.* parent[] is a forest; size[root] = number of vertices in its tree; `components` = number of roots;
`largest` = max size over all roots (sizes only grow, so a running max is exact).
*Correctness.* A union changes only the two merged sets; the answer after each operation is computed from exactly these invariants.
*Boundaries.* n = 0 and no operations → empty `states`; u == v; repeated pairs; n = 1 → largest = 1.
*Complexity.* O((n + q) · α(n)) time, O(n + q) memory (the q output states).

## Kruskal (`kruskal`)

*Steps.* Pack every edge into one `long` key `(w + 10⁹) << 20 | id`, sort the primitive array (weight, then id),
scan it with DSU, take an edge if its endpoints are in different sets; `components = n − taken`.
*Invariants.* The taken edges form a forest; every skipped edge connects two vertices already connected by cheaper-or-equal taken edges.
*Correctness.* Cut/cycle property: the cheapest edge crossing any cut separating two components is safe, so greedy order is optimal;
loops are skipped automatically (same root); parallel edges — the later one is skipped. Negative weights are fine because only the order matters.
*Boundaries.* Loops, parallel edges, negative weights, isolates (counted in `components`), m = 0, n = 0. `id < 2²⁰` because m ≤ 250 000.
*Complexity.* O(m log m + m·α(n)) time, O(n + m) memory.

## Prim (`prim`)

*Steps.* Build undirected adjacency (loops dropped). For each unvisited vertex `s` start a new component: mark `s`, push its
edges into a min-heap (packed weight/id), pop the cheapest edge; if both endpoints are visited it is stale, otherwise
the unvisited endpoint joins the tree, its cost and id are recorded and its edges are pushed. The component ends when the heap is empty;
the outer loop restarts at the next unvisited vertex (**restart per component** → minimum spanning *forest*).
*Invariants.* The visited set of the current component is a tree; the heap contains every edge leaving the visited set (plus stale ones).
*Correctness.* The popped non-stale edge is the cheapest edge crossing the cut (visited, unvisited) → safe edge (cut property).
*Boundaries.* Disconnected graphs (restart), isolates (a component with no edges), loops, parallel edges, negative weights, m = 0.
*Complexity.* O(m log m) time (heap holds ≤ 2m entries, log 2m = O(log m)), O(n + m) memory.

## SCC (`scc`, Kosaraju)

*Steps.* (1) Iterative DFS on the original graph, record vertices in finish order. (2) Build the transpose; process vertices in
decreasing finish time, each unlabelled vertex starts a DFS on the transpose and everything it reaches gets the new label.
(3) For every edge `(u,v)` with `comp[u] != comp[v]` add `(comp[u], comp[v])` once (a `HashSet<Long>` of `a·k + b` removes duplicates).
*Invariants.* After pass 1, the vertex with the largest finish time lies in a source component of the condensation;
in pass 2 the transpose DFS cannot leave its component, because edges to other components point the "wrong way" in the transpose.
*Correctness.* Standard Kosaraju argument: for components C→C′, max finish(C) > max finish(C′).
*Boundaries.* Self-loops (same component, no pair), duplicate cross edges (one pair), isolates (singleton components), n = 0, deep chain, one giant cycle.
*Complexity.* O(n + m) time and memory.

## Topological order (`topo`)

*Steps.* Three states: white (0), gray (1, on the DFS stack), black (2, finished). An edge to a gray vertex is a back edge = directed cycle
(a self-loop is the shortest case) → return `acyclic=false, order=[]`. Otherwise vertices are written into the result array from the back in
finish order, i.e. the result is the reverse finish order.
*Invariants.* Gray vertices form a path from the DFS root; a vertex becomes black only after all its successors are black.
*Correctness.* No cycle ⇒ every edge `u→v` is explored when v is black or white, so v finishes before u ⇒ u precedes v in the reverse order.
A cycle always contains a back edge, so it is detected wherever it is in the graph (all vertices are used as roots).
*Boundaries.* n = 0, isolates, parallel edges, self-loop, cycle in a part that is not reachable from vertex 0, deep chain.
*Complexity.* O(n + m) time and memory.

## DAG shortest paths (`dag`)

*Steps.* (1) Run the same three-state DFS on the **whole** graph; a cycle anywhere (even unreachable from `source`) → `false`, two empty arrays.
(2) Otherwise, in topological order relax the outgoing edges of every *reached* vertex: `dist[v] + w < dist[to]` (strict) updates `dist` and `parent_edge[to] = original edge id`.
Unreached vertices keep `null` / `-1`; the source has `0` / `-1`.
*Invariants.* When v is processed, all its predecessors were processed before (topological order), so `dist[v]` is final.
*Correctness.* Induction along the topological order; negative weights are fine because each edge is relaxed exactly once from a final distance.
`parent_edge[v]` always enters v and satisfies `dist[parent] + w = dist[v]`, so following it gives a shortest route that ends at the source.
Strict `<` means the first best edge wins ties, parallel edges are handled by their ids.
*Boundaries.* n = 1, source with no edges, unreachable vertices, unreachable cycle, self-loop, 64-bit distances, deep chain.
*Complexity.* O(n + m) time and memory.

### Route-reconstruction helper

`Algorithms.route(parentEdge, edges, source, target)` returns the vertex route (follow `parent_edge`, read `edges[id][0]`, reverse);
`Algorithms.routeEdges(...)` returns the same route as original edge ids. Target == source → `[source]` (no edges); unreachable → empty list.
A guard of n steps protects against corrupt parent arrays. Time/memory O(route length).

Example graph (`tests/AlgorithmsTests.java`): n = 5, source 0, edges
`0:[0,1,2] 1:[0,1,2] 2:[0,2,1] 3:[1,3,-3] 4:[2,3,-2]`; result `distance=[0,2,1,-1,null]`, `parent_edge=[-1,0,2,4,-1]`.

| Call | Result | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `route(parent, edges, 0, 3)` (reachable) | `[0, 2, 3]` | 0→2 (1) and 2→3 (−2) = −1 = distance[3] |
| `route(parent, edges, 0, 0)` (source) | `[0]` | no edges needed |
| `route(parent, edges, 0, 4)` (unreachable) | `[]` | `parent_edge[4] = -1`, vertex 4 ≠ source |
| `routeEdges(parent, edges, 0, 1)` (parallel edges 0 and 1, both cost 2) | `[0]` | either id is valid; the first one wins because of strict `<`; the test accepts both |
| `route(parent, edges, 0, 3)` with the tie (via 1 or via 2, both −1) | `[0, 2, 3]` or `[0, 1, 3]` | both are shortest; the test only checks validity |

