## Benchmark: Kruskal vs Prim

*Inputs.* `Benchmark.java`, seed `20260 + n`; random connected graph (random spanning tree + random extra edges), weights in [−10⁶, 10⁶];
the same edge list is given to both methods. Sizes: (n, m) = (1 000, 5 000), (10 000, 50 000), (100 000, 250 000).
*Timer / boundary.* `System.nanoTime()` around the single call `Algorithms.kruskal(input)` / `Algorithms.prim(input)`;
the input is built before the timer; JSON parsing, printing and compilation are excluded.
*Protocol.* 3 untimed warm-up calls, then 5 measured runs per method and size; the median is reported.
*Command.* `java -Xmx384m -cp .build/tests Benchmark`.
*Environment.* Intel Xeon @ 2.10 GHz, 1 logical CPU available to the JVM, 4 GB RAM, Linux 6.18, OpenJDK 21.0.12 (compiled with `--release 17`), run with `-Xmx384m`. Timings are in milliseconds; a shared virtual machine was used, so single runs are noisy and the median is the reference value.

| n | m | Method | run 1 | run 2 | run 3 | run 4 | run 5 | median (ms) |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 000 | 5 000 | Kruskal | 2.00 | 6.81 | 3.75 | 1.26 | 1.20 | 2.00 |
| 1 000 | 5 000 | Prim | 12.94 | 3.19 | 8.17 | 7.61 | 7.74 | 7.74 |
| 10 000 | 50 000 | Kruskal | 15.59 | 17.91 | 20.02 | 15.87 | 14.68 | 15.87 |
| 10 000 | 50 000 | Prim | 53.67 | 36.43 | 30.87 | 32.37 | 42.41 | 36.43 |
| 100 000 | 250 000 | Kruskal | 43.30 | 91.61 | 38.69 | 71.21 | 39.69 | 43.30 |
| 100 000 | 250 000 | Prim | 484.38 | 374.47 | 465.45 | 663.52 | 335.20 | 465.45 |

*Discussion.* Both methods are O(m log m) and return the same total cost on every graph (column `cost` of the benchmark output).
Medians: Kruskal is faster at all sizes, about 3.9 times at n = 1 000, 2.3 times at n = 10 000 and 10.7 times at n = 100 000.
Kruskal sorts one primitive `long[]` and then performs cheap DSU operations. Prim builds adjacency lists and pushes up to 2m entries
into a `PriorityQueue<Long>` with boxing, so its constant factor is larger and memory traffic grows faster on the largest graph.
The ratio at the smallest sizes is influenced by JIT compilation and timer noise, so only the order (Kruskal faster) is reliable there.


